<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pompey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">106–<a href="/years/48-bce">48 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pompey was a Roman general and statesman of the first century BCE. During his eastern campaigns he annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 63 BCE. This ended the remnant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> and made Syria a Roman province.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pompey.jpg" alt="Pompey" width="277" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Didier Descouens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Venice)_Pompey_the_Great,_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gnaeus Pompeius Magnus was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picenum">Picenum</a>, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-pompeius-strabo">Gnaeus Pompeius Strabo</a>, who was the first of his family branch to reach senatorial status and became consul in 89 BCE. Pompey began his career serving with his father in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> died in 87 BCE, Pompey stood trial for his father's alleged embezzlement and was acquitted. In 83 BCE he raised a legion from his father's clients and veterans and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>, who greeted him with the title imperator.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In Sulla's civil war Pompey defeated the Marian forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. His opponents nicknamed him adulescentulus carnifex, the young butcher, for the execution of Carbo, while his troops called him Magnus, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. He later commanded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-sertorius">Quintus Sertorius</a> and arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in time to massacre 6,000 fugitives from the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartacus">Spartacus</a>. He was consul with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Marcus Licinius Crassus</a> in 70 BCE, when the tribunes recovered powers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> had removed.</p><p>Under the Lex Gabinia he was given wide powers to suppress the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and under the lex Manilia he took over the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a>. He then reorganised the East and intervened in Judea, backing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyrcanus-ii">Hyrcanus II</a> against his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristobulus-ii">Aristobulus II</a>. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> he held a third triumph in 61 BCE, but the Senate refused to ratify his eastern settlement. In 60 BCE he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> in the First Triumvirate and married Caesar's daughter Julia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Julia died in 54 BCE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carrhae">Carrhae</a> in 53 BCE, removing the ties that held the alliance. Pompey aligned himself with the optimates, was elected sole consul in 52 BCE, and in 49 BCE war broke out with Caesar. Defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pharsalus">Battle of Pharsalus</a> in 48 BCE, he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and was killed on 28 September 48 BCE at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelusium">Pelusium</a> by Lucius Septimius, a Roman officer serving the Egyptian army.</p><p>Ancient and later writers treated his fall as a great subject. Lucan retold the civil war in his epic De Bello Civili, now known as the Pharsalia, and Renaissance English and French dramatists returned to the story, among them George Chapman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-corneille">Pierre Corneille</a>. Pompey's money also built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> of Pompey, one of the best known structures of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pompey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first king known by name is Oroeses, who in 66–65 BCE attacked the Roman general <b>Pompey</b> during his Caucasian campaign and was defeated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The final phase came in the Sertorian War of 80–72 BCE, when the Roman rebel Quintus Sertorius won the support of Lusitanians and Celtiberians and resisted the armies of Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius and <b>Pompey</b> for several years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome conquered the empire in 69 BCE, and the defeat of Armenia's ally Mithridates VI of Pontus by <b>Pompey</b> at the Battle of the Lycus made the kingdom a Roman client in 66 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Mithridates VI of Pontus quelled an uprising in the region in 83 BCE, and <b>Pompey</b> occupied Colchis after Mithridates' defeat in 65 BCE, capturing the local chief Olthaces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-colchis">Kingdom of Colchis · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pompey the Great</b> fought his way into Iberia in 65 BCE while chasing Mithridates VI of Pontus, defeating King Artoces in battles on the Mtkvari and Aragvi rivers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Third Mithridatic War Lucullus broke his siege of Cyzicus in 73–72 BCE and defeated him at Cabira, and <b>Pompey</b> crushed his army on the Lycus river in 66 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picenum">Regio V Picenum</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelusium">Pelusium</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.05,32.6&amp;year=-69" aria-label="Pelusium on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Pompey"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5190 4908h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5190 4908h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=31.05,32.6&amp;year=-69">What was here in 69 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-pompeius-strabo"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Gnaeus Pompeius Strabo</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; d. 87 BCE</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/sextus-pompey"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sextus-pompey.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Sextus Pompey</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; c. 67–35 BCE</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;What! will you never cease prating of laws to us that have swords by our sides?&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Pompey &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; to the Mamertines of Messana, who pleaded their ancient privileges &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…whenever I stamp with my foot in any part of Italy, there will rise up forces enough in an instant, both horse and foot.&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Pompey &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; to those who asked what forces could stop Caesar, 50 BCE &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…more worshiped the rising than the setting sun…&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Pompey &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; Plutarch's account of his words to Sulla, who had refused him a triumph &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It behoves us, who are mortals born, to endure these events, and to try fortune yet again; neither is it any less possible to recover our former state, than it was to fall from that into this.&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Pompey &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; to his wife Cornelia after the defeat at Pharsalus; Plutarch's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
